 "1912"] "\n" [:option {:value "57191"} "1920"] "\n" [:option {:value "57471"} "1924"] "\n" [:option {:value "57753"} "1928"] "\n" [:option {:value "58026"} "1932"] "\n" [:option {:value "58275"} "1936"] "\n" [:option {:value "58627"} "1948"] "\n" [:option {:value "58997"} "1952"] "\n" [:option {:value "59376"} "1956"] "\n" [:option {:value "59773"} "1960"] "\n" [:option {:value "60207"} "1964"] "\n" [:option {:value "60644"} "1968"] "\n" [:option {:value "61162"} "1972"] "\n" [:option {:value "61738"} "1976"] "\n" [:option {:value "62299"} "1980"] "\n" [:option {:value "62791"} "1984"] "\n" [:option {:value "63433"} "1988"] "\n" [:option {:value "64109"} "1992"] "\n" [:option {:value "64801"} "1996"] "\n" [:option {:value "65470"} "2000"] "\n" [:option {:value "66143"} "2004"] "\n" [:option {:value "257275"} "2008"] "\n" [:option {:value "302248"} "2012"] "\n" [:option {:value "358684"} "2016"] "\n" [:option {:value "19000482"} "2020"] "\n" [:option {:value "2013639"} "2024"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/73"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "High Jump, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "21 July 1908"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/216"} "White City Stadium, London"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/56443"} "22 from 10 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The high jump world record was still held by America’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/891538"} "Mike Sweeney"] ", who had cleared 6-5 5/8 (1.97) in 1895.  He had long since turned professional and subsequently retired.  The Olympic record was the 6-2­ (1.90) which had won the " [:a {:href "/results/56090"} "1900"] " gold medal for " [:a {:href "/athletes/78090"} "Irv Baxter"] " (USA).  The favorite for the 1908 Olympics was probably the great Irish jumper " [:a {:href "/athletes/69237"} "Con Leahy"] ", who had won the AAA title from 1905-08.  He had also won the American AAU championship in 1907, and was also the defending Olympic champion, having won in " [:a {:href "/results/56223"} "1906"] ".  The best American jumper was " [:a {:href "/athletes/78909"} "Harry Porter"] ", who in 1908 won the AAU title and the Eastern Olympic Trial.  Porter had also been 2nd at the 1905 IC4A Meet."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The " [:strong {} "Official Report"] " makes no mention of qualifying conditions, but there were eight scheduled qualifying sections which were consolidated into four pools.  After Pool One had been completed at the South End of the Stadium, the officials decided that the slippery conditions were unsuitable and moved the remaining three pools to another jumping area at the North End of the Stadium.  " [:a {:href "/athletes/78451"} "Herbert Gidney"] " (USA), who had not qualified in the first pool, then lodged a protest, claiming that the original results of the first pool should be declared void and the competition held again under more favorable conditions at the North End.  Despite the fact that all the competitors had been equally affected by the original adverse conditions, the judges, rather surprisingly, upheld Gidney’s protest."] "\n\n" [:p {} [:a {:href "/athletes/74193"} "Otto Monsen"] " (NOR) and " [:a {:href "/athletes/69235"} "Edward Leader"] " (GBR) had shared first place in the original competition, but Monsen refused to take part in the re-scheduled event, while Leader failed to match the height he had achieved in the less favorable conditions.  The only beneficiary of the protest was Gidney himself."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Having won the competition, Harry Porter made three unsuccessful attempts at the world record height of 6-5 5/8­ (1.97). For these world record attempts, Porter removed his sweater for the first time in the competition.  In the final, " [:a {:href "/athletes/71282"} "István Somodi"] " (HUN) was the only competitor to improve on his qualifying mark in the morning.  Leahy, Somodi, and " [:a {:href "/athletes/68033"} "Géo André"] " (FRA) had a jump-off for the silver and bronze medals, but none could equal their earlier marks, so they were declared equal second."] "\n\n" [:p {} "After the Olympics, Harry Porter continued to be a top jumper.  He led the 1909 world lists with 6-4 (1.93), and tied for first in 1911 at the AAU Meet, but he did not compete at the " [:a {:href "/results/56702"} "1912 Olympics"] "."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Qualifying"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/78909"} "Harry Porter"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "1.90 (" [:span {:class "label label-primary"} [:abbrev {:title "No Placement"} "NP"]] ")"] [:td {} "1.90 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-default"} [:abbrev {:title "Olympic Record"} "OR"]]] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "=2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/69237"} "Con Leahy"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GBR.png"}] "GBR"]] [:td {} "1.88 (" [:span {:class "label label-primary"} [:abbrev {:title "No Placement"} "NP"]] ")"] [:td {} "1.88 (=2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "=2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/71282"} "István Somodi"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/HUN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/HUN.p